Helios DXVK fork: run a callback on the submission thread right after a frame's submission

Conduit guest/windows. Applied after 0018 by ci/windows/Build-Driver.ps1.

A composed D3D11 present on NVK hands the KMD an RM fence in its WDDM present
marker. The fence used to come from NVK's queue_rm_fence, which signals the
present timeline after what is already submitted to the queue, so the
presenting thread first waited in HeliosWaitFrameSubmitted until DXVK's CS
thread had executed the frame's flush and the submission thread had performed
its vkQueueSubmit (and lockSubmission then waited for the submission queue to
drain once more). CS2 spent about 9% of its render thread there.

With a fence for a value reserved up front (helios_icd_interface version 8,
ecl_fence_create_v3), only the timeline signal has to follow the frame on the
queue, and nobody has to wait for it:

- DxvkCommandList::heliosAfterSubmit / heliosRunAfterSubmit: callbacks the
  submission thread runs right after the list's vkQueueSubmit succeeded, with
  the device queue still locked (dropped unrun on a failed submission).
- DxvkContext::heliosAfterSubmit: attach one to the list being recorded.
- D3D11ImmediateContext::HeliosSubmitFrameThen: emit a CS command that attaches
  the callback to the current list, then flush it. The callback runs after the
  submission that carries everything recorded so far; the presenting thread
  returns at once.

diff --git a/src/d3d11/d3d11_context_imm.cpp b/src/d3d11/d3d11_context_imm.cpp
index 4226b4b7..5019cf8b 100644
--- a/src/d3d11/d3d11_context_imm.cpp
+++ b/src/d3d11/d3d11_context_imm.cpp
@@ -1214,6 +1214,28 @@ namespace dxvk {
   }
 
 
+  bool D3D11ImmediateContext::HeliosSubmitFrameThen(std::function<void ()>&& fn) {
+    D3D10DeviceLock lock = LockContext();
+
+    if (m_device->getDeviceStatus() != VK_SUCCESS)
+      return false;
+
+    // A command that hands `fn` to the command list the CS thread is
+    // recording, then the flush that submits that list: everything the app
+    // recorded since the last flush goes in the same submission, and the
+    // submission thread runs `fn` right after it. A frame already flushed
+    // before this call is earlier on the queue anyway.
+    EmitCs([
+      cFn = std::move(fn)
+    ] (DxvkContext* ctx) mutable {
+      ctx->heliosAfterSubmit(std::move(cFn));
+    });
+
+    ExecuteFlush(GpuFlushType::ExplicitFlush, nullptr, false);
+    return m_device->getDeviceStatus() == VK_SUCCESS;
+  }
+
